-- title:
--   Minimal primes of a flat algebra meet A[x] trivially
-- statement:
--   Let $A$ be a commutative domain with fraction field $L$ (i.e. $L$ is a field, an $A$-algebra, and the structure map makes $L$ a localisation of $A$ at its non-zero divisors), let $B$ be a commutative $A$-algebra that is flat as an $A$-module, and let $x \in B$. Assume that $B$ is integral over the $A$-subalgebra $A[x] =$ `Algebra.adjoin A {x}` generated by $x$, and assume that no minimal prime of the zero ideal of $L \otimes_A B$ is a maximal ideal. The conclusion is that for every minimal prime $\mathfrak p$ of the zero ideal of $B$, the contraction of $\mathfrak p$ along the inclusion $A[x] \hookrightarrow B$ is the zero ideal of $A[x]$; equivalently, $\mathfrak p \cap A[x] = 0$, so that no non-zero element of $A[x]$ lies in $\mathfrak p$.
--
--   An elementary commutative-algebra statement: flatness over the domain $A$ forces minimal primes of $B$ to lie over $(0)$, and the hypothesis that the generic fibre $L \otimes_A B$ has no isolated (maximal minimal) prime rules out any algebraic relation for $x$ modulo such a prime. It is used in the study of modular curves, where it is applied to the subalgebra generated by a $j$-invariant-type element to show that minimal primes of certain rings of functions contract to zero.
-- source:
--   https://github.com/anthropics/fermats-last-theorem/blob/aa2d8b34692b16c70f699536de0d8e75b9a3e9ef/Theorems/Thm_Ideal_comap_adjoin_singleton_eq_bot_of_mem_minimalPrimes_of_flat_of_isIntegral.lean

